  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened institutional capacity of employers&apos; organizations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Effective labour market governance requires strong, independent and democratic employers&#x2019; organisations and effective, accountable and inclusive labour administration systems, based on sound labour laws and social dialogue. The impact of these institutions goes beyond the workplace as they also contribute to wider economic, social and environmental progress and promote peaceful, resilient and inclusive societies. The interventions is focusing on providing entry points for ILO engagement with the private sector, including through initiatives such as the ILO Global Business Network on Forced Labour and increasing their representativeness and their ability to speak on behalf of the private sector as a whole, advocating for evidence-based, data-driven policy reforms and leading improvements in the business climate and productivity growth through research and communication to put forward the priorities of the private sector and to support the wider development agenda, becoming high performing, well-governed and resilient organizations through organizational scans, capability assessments and human resource development, developing high-impact, needs-driven services, fostering long-term business competitiveness, addressing environmental, social and governance challenges, becoming data-driven organizations by promoting a culture of innovation that positions data at the centre of the employers&#x2019; organization strategy and decision-making and participating and influencing United Nations development processes, including United Nations Cooperation Framework and annual updates of the Common Country Analysis.  
Beneficiaries of this intervention include the employers of Malawi especially those in the private sector. 
The intervention is being implemented in collaboration with Employers&#x2019; Organizations, Workers&#x2019; Organisations, sector Associations, private sector players and the Government of Malawi.</narrative>
  </description>
